    {
        "content": "<p>Is this not true in general?</p>",
        "id": 587832850,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Mure",
        "timestamp": 1776709371
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>See e.g. <a class=\"message-link\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/Puzzle.3A.20Decidable.20HEq/near/536764271\">#lean4 &gt; Puzzle: Decidable HEq @ 💬</a>  for counterexamples</p>",
        "id": 587832983,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1776709412
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In general it's impossible to prove that two types in Lean are unequal if they have the same cardinality; indeed there's a \"cardinality model\" of Lean where there's exactly one type of each cardinality. You're lucky in the <code>Fin</code> case because the types have provably distinct cardinalities.</p>",
        "id": 587833541,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1776709611
    }
]